 "conclusions": [
  "The 5 ms variant completed 193/200 loads versus 182/200 for 30 ms; paired successful-load median latency changed by -0.514 s. The site-cluster success-difference interval is 0 to 12.5 percentage points.",
  "Full-trace legacy scores were 8.85% (30 ms) and 10.61% (5 ms). Whole-round holdout scores were 10.94% and 15.15%, versus nominal 5% chance. Both exceeded all 100 round-block label-map null scores; Holm adjustment of the two full-trace tests gives p=0.0198 each.",
  "Across all six tested views the smallest Holm-adjusted p is 0.0594, so the directional results do not meet a six-view family-wise 5% threshold with this finite permutation budget.",
  "Classification signal remains after excluding loads whose final protection snapshot was not protected. A final snapshot does not prove continuous protection of the complete window.",
  "All captures used one repeated site/repeat order and a fixed variant order, with no fresh undefended control. Round holdout does not remove a repeated-order artifact. These data do not establish causal leakage differences or general privacy.",
  "The relay skipped about 0.572% of its clock slots at 5 ms versus 0.00145% at 30 ms. This is consistent with less tolerance of scheduling stalls, not proof of the cause of classifier scores, and is not necessarily data-packet loss.",
  "Do not promote 5 ms or enable the experimental wire clock by default from these results. Prepare a fresh-control, counterbalanced and varied-seed collection first."
